{"seq": 160, "ts": "2026-11-22T02:45:57.589610+00:00", "run_id": "20261122-024215-7904d4", "type": "message", "role": "agent", "author": "ChipIn", "text": "I chose the 2-feature integer logistic chip (`1b05ecf2359a`, 237 gates). On the two held-out patients it caught 8 of 8 seizures with 0.17 false alarms per hour, so it meets both targets (sensitivity ≥ 0.8, at most 1 false alarm per hour). All of this is on **synthetic data**, not patient recordings.\n\n- **Line-length threshold (51 gates): failed.** It caught 0 seizures, because in my simulated data muscle (EMG) bursts have higher line length than seizures.\n- **Energy threshold (70 gates): failed the false-alarm target.** It caught every seizure but gave 1.5 false alarms per hour, set off by motion artefacts.\n- **The chip computes exactly what its model does.** All three chips matched their models bit for bit on 10,800 held-out windows and were formally proved equal (verification level 2).\n- **What is on the chip so far:** only the decision step is compiled to gates. Two other parts run in software: computing the window features, and the rule that raises an alarm only after 4 positive 2-second windows in a row.\n- **What I skipped:** layout and timing/power, because Docker isn't available here. So there is no measured power figure; the chip lab estimates the area at about 1,640 µm² on SkyWater 130 nm, before layout.\n- **The test set is small:** 8 seizures in 6 hours, so sensitivity can only move in steps of 0.125.\n\nThe next step would be to run the same pipeline on a public EEG seizure dataset using two channels, and to move the feature step and the alarm counter onto the chip.\n\nFiles are in the working folder:\n- report.md\n- spec.md\n- data/assumptions.md"}
